<!--
  Three captured frames in a cascade, each brighter than the one behind it, the front
  one pierced by a lens. That is what the app does: it holds the camera fixed and takes
  a whole series of frames, one per point in the settings grid.

  Aperture and lens-dial motifs were tried first and dropped. An iris is the most
  camera-like mark available, but aperture is the one parameter this fixed f/1.8 rig
  cannot sweep, so it would advertise a feature that does not exist.

  All three frames carry the same evenOdd cut-out so the lens punches through to the
  background gradient. Cutting only the front frame exposes the one behind it and the
  lens fills with a half-tone instead of reading as an opening.

  Frame corners reach 31.5 of the 36 unit round-mask safe radius, so nothing clips.
-->
